 ## Background
-The shift from software-based ray casting to hardware-accelerated implementations was driven by the need for real-time interaction with large volumetric datasets. Early hardware-accelerated approaches leveraged the fixed-function pipeline's texture units for interpolation, while modern implementations utilize programmable fragment shaders for complex compositing, lighting, and deformation [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md]. The field has expanded to include multi-GPU strategies for neural volume rendering (NeRFs) and specialized handling for unstructured meshes and AMR data [S1, S2, S3].
+The shift from software-based ray casting to hardware acceleration was driven by the need for interactive frame rates. Early implementations utilized fixed-function pipeline features, such as 3D texture mapping and alpha blending, to perform the ray marching loop in the fragment shader [5616-hardware-accelerated-volume-rendering.md]. As programmable shaders became available, more complex techniques like pre-integration, lighting, and multi-volume compositing were integrated directly into the GPU pipeline [1184-direct-volume-rendering.md][2461-gpu-multi-volume-ray-casting-mapper.md]. The approach has since expanded to handle unstructured grids [9631-gpu-based-tiled-ray-casting-with-depth-peeling.md], adaptive mesh refinement [10503-hardware-accelerated-amr-volume-rendering-via-homogenization.md], and even neural radiance fields (NeRFs) across multiple GPUs [S2].
 
